The header "B-ave" suggests the average of column B for the same country. The prefix is only `=`, so I'm assuming the user wants the average of column B for rows matching A2.

The simplest option is `=AVERAGEIF($A$2:$A$11,A2,$B$2:$B$11)`.

{"formula": "=AVERAGEIF($A$2:$A$11,A2,$B$2:$B$11)"}

Headwind component is wind speed times the cosine of the angle between runway heading and wind direction. The existing crosswind formula in X10 is =ABS(X8*SIN(DEGREES(U7-X7))), which treats the angle as radians converted to degrees. That is odd, but I'll match its convention for consistency. Headwind would then be X8*COS(DEGREES(U7-X7)).

The correct version would use RADIANS, but the user's crosswind uses DEGREES, so I'll follow their pattern.

{"formula": "=X8*COS(DEGREES(U7-X7))"}

Column G is titled "Absolute Value of Purchase", and the Payment column F only returns positive amounts. So G most likely shows the absolute value of the negative amounts (the purchases) and leaves positive amounts blank.

The formula mirrors the one in F: `=IF(E2<0,ABS(E2),"")`.

{"formula": "=IF(E2<0,ABS(E2),\"\")"}

The V column is "28 day range" in MPa, so the formula should return the spread of the 28-day results in Q6:S6. S6 holds "-", which MAX and MIN ignore when they take a range reference, so a range works here.

The cell is empty and only the prefix `=` has been typed. The best completion is the maximum minus the minimum across Q6:S6.

{"formula": "=MAX(Q6:S6)-MIN(Q6:S6)"}

The neighboring formula N2 is `=ABS(E2-K2)`, so M2 most likely computes the time difference in the same way: `=ABS(D2-J2)`.

The time values in D and J are text strings such as "Jan 5 2012 12:15:32.12", so subtracting them directly may or may not work. I'm assuming they are real date-times, which makes the direct subtraction the pattern that matches N2.

{"formula": "=ABS(D2-J2)"}

Column O is labeled "Error" with the unit "( OF )", which most likely means an objective function. In this setup, G is the experimental pressure in kPa and N is the calculated pressure in kPa. The error is therefore most likely the squared difference between them. The ARD column in P would then be the absolute relative deviation in percent.

An objective function in this kind of work is commonly the sum of squared differences, so a squared difference per row fits. A relative form such as ((G13-N13)/G13)^2 is also possible. I chose the simple squared difference.

{"formula": "=(G13-N13)^2"}

The Price in J5 should be the minimum price for the part number in H5. I5 looks up the person by matching the part number and the price in J5, so J5 has to hold the minimum price for that part.

A MINIFS formula works here: `=MINIFS(D$3:D$21,$B$3:$B$21,$H5)`. The I5 formula uses `C$3:C$21` with mixed references, so I'm matching that style. MINIFS is available in Excel 2019 and later.

{"formula": "=MINIFS(D$3:D$21,$B$3:$B$21,$H5)"}

The column G header is "Reported month", and the formulas elsewhere use G as a TRUE/FALSE flag. K3 takes MAXIFS over A where G is TRUE, and H uses IF(G4, ...). So G4 should return TRUE when the month has an actual entered, which is `=E4<>""`.

An alternative is `=ISNUMBER(E4)`, but `=E4<>""` is simpler and more common.

{"formula": "=E4<>\"\""}

F47 is Theta, and F46 already holds x/y = (B47-D8)/(D11-B48), which is tan(Θ). F48 converts F47 from radians to degrees, so F47 must be in radians. That makes the formula the arctangent of F46.

The later formulas support this: F52 uses COS(F47), F67 uses COS(F47), and F68 uses SIN(F47/2), all of which treat F47 as radians.

{"formula": "=ATAN(F46)"}

Cell I8 is labelled "New run rate last 14 days", so O8 should sum the last 14 days of the New row (row 3) and divide by 14, mirroring the month-to-date formula in F8. Day *d* sits in the column *d* places to the right of column A, and A6 holds the current day of the month. That makes the 14-day window start at an offset of A6-13 columns from A3.

This assumes today is at least the 14th. For earlier days the offset goes to zero or negative, so the window would run off the start of the data.

{"formula": "=SUM(OFFSET(A3,0,A6-13,1,14))/14"}

The label in M3 reads "Average of highest 7 in Column L", and P3 is the target cell next to it. The formula therefore averages the 7 largest values in L3:L454, and the prefix is just `=`.

`LARGE` accepts an array constant for k, so `SUMPRODUCT` can add the seven largest values and divide by 7. `AVERAGE` on its own would also work with the same array constant.

{"formula": "=AVERAGE(LARGE($L$3:$L$454,{1,2,3,4,5,6,7}))"}

I50 is the PM tons/yr value for the cement silo, after controls/limit, in the potential-emissions block. It should follow the same pattern as I46 and I47, which are H's lb/hr formula multiplied by 8760/2000.

H50 is `=CHOOSE(INPUT!$F$23,INPUT!$F$22,FACTORS!$C$44)*2.012*FACTORS!$B$5*INPUT!$F$35/INPUT!$F$40`, so I50 is that expression with `*8760/2000` appended.

{"formula": "=CHOOSE(INPUT!$F$23,INPUT!$F$22,FACTORS!$C$44)*2.012*FACTORS!$B$5*INPUT!$F$35/INPUT!$F$40*8760/2000"}

The cell above, F60, holds the sine formula `=SIN(RADIANS(H57+H58))`. F61 is labelled "cosw", so the matching cosine formula is most likely what the user wants.

The sine formula refers to H57 and H58, although the nearby data sits in column F, where F59 holds w = F57+F58. I can't tell whether the H references are an error. Following the existing pattern is the safest choice, so I keep the same references for consistency.

{"formula": "=COS(RADIANS(H57+H58))"}

The cell B25 sits in the row for person A (row 4 in the schedule), and B24 holds the shift start time 06:00. I took the intent to be a count of how many days person A's shift starts at that time. The formula formats the start time as text and counts shifts that begin with it.

For the "Off" column (K24), `TEXT` returns the text unchanged, so it counts every entry that begins with "OFF". That includes variants such as "OFF*" and "OFF/VAC*". This is my best guess from the layout, since the prefix is only `=`.

{"formula": "=COUNTIF($B4:$CN4,TEXT(B$24,\"hh:mm\")&\"*\")"}

The column header is "Years of Service" and B7 holds the date joined. The formula should return whole years of service, and `DATEDIF` does that. The end date is not given, so I am assuming `TODAY()`.

The `D` column already feeds this value into `LOOKUP(C7,{1,10},...)`, which expects a number of whole years.

{"formula": "=DATEDIF(B7,TODAY(),\"y\")"}

The target cell is D6, "Years Employed". Column C holds the hire date, and Z6 holds the "as of" date (2013-07-01), labelled in Y6 as "Years Employed as of". Years employed is therefore the difference between Z6 and C6.

`DATEDIF` returns whole completed years, which fits the later test `D6>=1` in the Family Leave column. Z6 is anchored as `$Z$6` so the formula can be filled down.

{"formula": "=DATEDIF(C6,$Z$6,\"y\")"}

G2 is "Days from lease start to today", and J1 holds the date used in the duration calculation (2023-11-08). Lease start is the Commencement Date in D3. The days from lease start to today would be $J$1-D3.

The H column then uses DATEDIF(0,G3,"y"), which treats G3 as a day count, so the formula should return a plain number of days.

{"formula": "=$J$1-D3"}

The end date is the start date plus the tenure in years, minus one day. Using EDATE gives `=EDATE(B3,C3*12)-1`.

Rent starting 2018-02-15 with a 20-year tenure ends 2038-02-14, which is what this formula returns.

{"formula": "=EDATE(B3,C3*12)-1"}

Row 23 holds "Forecast", and N22 holds X = 1185. The model is log-log: Ln Y = Slope * Ln X + InterCept. The forecast of Y is therefore EXP(LN(N22)*Slope+InterCept).

The named ranges Slope and InterCept are already used in column H, so I'll reuse them.

{"formula": "=EXP(LN(N22)*Slope+InterCept)"}

Column P is labeled DELTA. For a call option, delta is N(d1), and N2 holds d1. Since the dividend yield is in column O, the delta formula would be EXP(-O2*M2)*NORM.S.DIST(N2,TRUE). This matches the Gamma formula in Q2, which already includes EXP(-1*M2*O2).

The user's prefix is only "=", so I'm writing the full formula in the same style as Q2.

{"formula": "=EXP(-1*M2*O2)*NORM.S.DIST(N2,TRUE)"}

The Discount Rate row sits between Time (row 13) and Cash Flows (row 15), and the PV formulas use EXP(-rate*time). That points to a continuous-compounding discount factor, with the YTM in C10 as the rate. The existing PV formulas reference C10, D10 and E10 without anchoring, which looks like a mistake, so I'll anchor C10 here.

The cell I14 likely holds the discount factor, `=EXP(-$C$10*I13)`. It could instead hold just the rate, `=$C$10`. Since the row is labeled "Discount Rate" and I16 multiplies the cash flow by EXP(-C10*I13) itself, the rate is the more literal reading, but the factor is the more useful content for a row between Time and Cash Flows. I'll go with the rate, `=$C$10`, to match the label.

{"formula": "=$C$10"}

The column is "Time Rounded", and C holds the time as a fraction of a day. Rounding that to the nearest hour (a 1-hour step) fits the data, since the times are about an hour apart. MROUND(C2,"1:00") would work, but ROUND(C2*24,0)/24 is more robust because it avoids MROUND's text-time handling.

The Day/Night test in column E compares against 07:00, so the rounding must produce a time of day. A value that rounds up to 24:00 would be 1, which would count as DAY. The data has times like 23:59:58 that round to 24:00, and these should count as NIGHT. Using MOD(...,1) turns 1 into 0, which gives NIGHT, so I'll use that.

{"formula": "=MOD(ROUND(C2*24,0)/24,1)"}

Cell C9 sits in the Baggage Arm row, between B9 (value 0) and D9 and E9. D9 and E9 each hold `=FORECAST(D8,$E$13:$E$14,$D$13:$D$14)` and `=FORECAST(E8,$E$13:$E$14,$D$13:$D$14)`. The pattern is that each cell forecasts its own column's baggage weight from row 8, so C9 should use C8, which holds 50.

The pattern follows the neighbouring formulas exactly, so I'm completing it the same way.

{"formula": "=FORECAST(C8,$E$13:$E$14,$D$13:$D$14)"}

The formula pattern in F24:H27 is `=FV($B$17,COUNT($D$8:$D24)-1, 0, -$B$19*$E24, 0)`, so F8 most likely follows the same pattern with row 8 references.

The prefix is only `=`, so I'm filling the rest in from that pattern. The existing formulas include spaces after commas, and I'm keeping the same spacing.

{"formula": "=FV($B$17,COUNT($D$8:$D8)-1, 0, -$B$19*$E8, 0)"}

This is a best guess, since the sheet doesn't state the intended formula. I read E12 as the total price per m² under the 6-payment plan. I set it so that the present value of the 6 installments, discounted at C6, equals the cash price in D12. `PMT` gives the per-period installment, and multiplying by the number of periods in C7 gives the total.

I assumed payments are made at the end of each period and that the discount rate applies per period. If the first payment is due at the time of sale, you would add a type argument of 1 to `PMT`.

{"formula": "=-PMT($C$6,$C$7,D12)*$C$7"}

The Month(s) column in C17 is `=MOD(((SUM(B2:B7)*12+SUM(C2:C7))/ROWS(A2:A7)),12)`, so the Year(s) column in B17 should be the matching average in whole years: total months divided by the number of rows, divided by 12, with the decimals dropped.

I used INT to drop the decimals, and kept the same pattern as C17.

{"formula": "=INT(((SUM(B2:B7)*12+SUM(C2:C7))/ROWS(A2:A7))/12)"}

Column C holds Age, and the target C5 sits next to a birth date in B5. The existing values (18, 25, 44) look hard-coded and don't match the birth dates. The formulas elsewhere use 2024 as the reference year, so I'll compute age with DATEDIF against a fixed 2024 date or against TODAY().

The most likely intent is a standard age calculation: `=DATEDIF(B5,TODAY(),"Y")`.

{"formula": "=DATEDIF(B5,TODAY(),\"Y\")"}

Interest for the month is the balance times the monthly rate. The rate used in the PMT formulas is 0.73109%, and the balance is in B10. Check against row 4: 1650000 × 0.0073109 = 12063.0. That matches E4.

D10 already equals C10-E10, so E10 only needs the interest calculation.

{"formula": "=B10*0.73109%"}

The cell M7 sits under "Longest Warnings" and mirrors I7, which uses SMALL. The longest-warnings equivalent uses LARGE with the same IF condition and the rank in L7.

I7 uses `($B$7:$B$29>=$C$3)+($B$7:$B$29<=$E$3)`, which is an OR condition. I'm following the existing pattern for consistency.

{"formula": "=LARGE(IF(($B$7:$B$29>=$C$3)+($B$7:$B$29<=$E$3),$C$7:$C$29),L7)"}

I can't tell exactly what you intend, so this is my best guess: a turnaround time from the order date to the completion date, excluding weekends.

The vacation dates in column F are text such as "December 12 to December 22", so NETWORKDAYS can't use them as a holiday list. I left them out. If you meant the clock to start at the dictation date instead, the formula would use C2 in place of B2.

{"formula": "=NETWORKDAYS(B2,D2)"}

The Q column mirrors the N column inputs: Q2 = P13 (the outstanding principal after payment 4, used as the new loan), Q3 = N3, Q5 = N5, and Q6 = N6. Q4 is the Months input, which would follow the same pattern.

The loan was reset after 4 months, so the remaining months would be N4 minus 4. Row 13 corresponds to the 4th payment, so the remaining term is `=N4-L13`. The new schedule starts at row 17, which is payment 8, and runs through row 24, which is payment 15. That would imply a term of 15-8+1 = 8 months, or 12-4 = 8, so `=N4-L13` gives 8, which matches.

{"formula": "=N4-L13"}

F2 holds the Pearson R², and E3 is labeled "Pearson II". The most likely intent is a second way of computing the same R², for example with RSQ on the same ranges.

Another candidate is `=PEARSON(LN(B2:B17),A2:A17)^2`, which would parallel the "LINEST with Y transformed" approach. However, "Pearson II" most likely means the Pearson calculation applied to the transformed Y, so I chose that one.

{"formula": "=PEARSON(LN(B2:B17),A2:A17)^2"}

The row 2 formulas compute the monthly minimum, ignoring zeros. Cell G3 is the 25th percentile, so the same pattern applies with PERCENTILE in place of MIN. The month is matched through G$1 and zero values are excluded with the nested IF.

The percentile argument can be 0.25, as that is the row's label. This is the most likely intent and keeps the formula consistent with row 2.

{"formula": "=PERCENTILE(IF(MONTH($A$2:$A$731)=MONTH(DATEVALUE(G$1&\" 1\")),IF($B$2:$B$731<>0,$B$2:$B$731)),0.25)"}

The Principle column in F17 should be the payment minus the interest. PMT and IPMT both return negative values here, so `D17-E17` gives the (negative) principal portion. This matches G17 `=D6+F17`, where the balance is the loan amount plus the negative principal.

An alternative is `PPMT`, but the prefix is only `=`, and the sheet already uses D and E for the payment and interest.

{"formula": "=D17-E17"}

Column I is the Average column, so the cell should divide total pins by total games. In row 5, H5 holds total pins and F5 holds total games.

The existing formulas use mixed references like `$H5`, so I'll follow that style.


{"formula": "=$H5/$F5"}

D16 is labelled "sd" (E16), so it should hold the standard deviation of the weighted distribution. D15 already computes the mean as SUMPRODUCT(A11:A37,C11:C37)/H2, so the standard deviation is the square root of the weighted mean of squared deviations from that mean.

=SQRT(SUMPRODUCT(C11:C37,(A11:A37-D15)^2)/H2)

{"formula": "=SQRT(SUMPRODUCT(C11:C37,(A11:A37-D15)^2)/H2)"}

C46 sits directly below the last data row, so it most likely holds a grand total of the Full Plant Capacity column. Column C has per-plant SUMs in rows 8, 11, 13, 15, 18, 20, 24, 28, 30, 34, 38, 43 and 45. It also has hardcoded values in rows 31 and 32. Summing C5:C45 therefore gives the total, because SUM ignores the blank cells.

Alternatively, `=SUM(H5:H45)` would total the unit capacities directly. That is the same figure as the sum of column C, but the plant subtotals are the more natural thing to add up in column C.

{"formula": "=SUM(C5:C45)"}

Profit Factor is gross profit divided by gross loss. The gain/loss values are in M44:M112, and the existing formulas in C5 to C10 use that same range.

The user's other formulas use SUMIF on M44:M112, so I'll follow that pattern. Dividing the sum of gains by the absolute value of the sum of losses gives the profit factor.

{"formula": "=SUMIF(M44:M112,\">0\")/ABS(SUMIF(M44:M112,\"<0\"))"}

Magnesium sulphate is dosed at 40 mg/kg over 20 minutes, with the weight in C6. The other doses in this column are built as text with a unit, so I followed that pattern: `=C6*40&" mg"`.

Some magnesium doses have a maximum, such as 2 g (2000 mg). The sheet gives no max for this drug, so I left it out.

{"formula": "=C6*40&\" mg\""}

E6 is labeled "First Year", so it should return the budget for the first year of the period, which is the start date's year. The prefix is only "=", so the simplest formula that fits the context is the year of the start date in C2.

An alternative would be a budget lookup, but the label "First Year" sits beside a cell that most likely holds the year itself. Rows 7 and 8 ("Last Year", "Between") and the total in row 10 suggest a layout where the budget amounts are computed later.

{"formula": "=YEAR(C2)"}

I can't tell what you intend for I16, so this is a guess. I16 sits directly under the "Empty Trays" total in I15, and K15 holds the matching total for the second method. The most plausible use is the difference between the two totals:

{"formula": "=I15-K15"}

If you meant something else, such as a combined total or a different comparison, the formula would need to change.

Cell B8 sits under the "Month" header row, where B7 holds a selected month (2012-08-01) and A8 holds the vendor name (Vitro). The source data has vendors in A3:A5 and months in B2:L2, so a two-way lookup fits best.

The formula uses INDEX/MATCH, with the vendor reference locked to column A and the month reference locked to row 7 so it can be filled down and across.

{"formula": "=INDEX($B$3:$L$5,MATCH($A8,$A$3:$A$5,0),MATCH($B$7,$B$2:$L$2,0))"}

