J7 sits beside the center name in I7 ("ATL") and the top agent's name in K7. Row 4 already shows the pattern: J4 looks up the Agent Group of the top revenue figure in L4. I assumed J7 does the same for the top figure in L7, so it returns that agent's group using the same lookup as K7.

{"formula": "=INDEX($D$2:$D$60,MATCH(L7,$F$2:$F$60,0))"}

The start date of the next task is most likely the previous task's start date plus its duration in days. Column E holds the time to complete in working days (weeks × 5), so the end of the previous task in calendar terms would use WORKDAY.

`=WORKDAY(B7,E7)` adds E7 working days to the start date in B7. That is a reasonable guess because E is expressed in working days (D × 5).

{"formula": "=WORKDAY(B7,E7)"}

The column is "N-MSD", the moving standard deviation over N days. Column H already uses the window E2:E5 for the 4-day moving average, so I2 should use the same window with a standard deviation function.

Population or sample standard deviation is ambiguous. Bollinger-style bands usually use the population standard deviation, so I chose STDEV.P. The result is `=STDEV.P(E2:E5)`.

{"formula": "=STDEV.P(E2:E5)"}

The header "Log(1 - Reliability)" sits over J:L, so each cell should be the base-10 log of one minus the matching reliability in G:I. The labels in D use the same idea, since 1-10^C is the inverse of this transformation.

The formula is `=LOG10(1-G4)`, which fills right and down.

